^Plural^

In Finnish, when we say we have one of something, we usually just leave out the word ‘one’. If we want to say ‘there is a phone on the table’ we say *pöydällä on puhelin*, and we leave out the word ‘one’ (*yksi*). Saying *yksi puhelin* is used only when making it clear there is only one phone, as opposed to two or zero.

There is no word for ‘to have’ in Finnish. Instead, we say ‘on me is’, e.g. ‘on me is a phone’, *minulla on puhelin*

Minulla on (I have)
Sinulla on (you have)
Hänellä on (he/she has)
Meillä on (we have)
Teillä on (you all have)
Heillä on (they have)

Note: While there is a word for ‘to own’ in Finnish, it is used the same way as in English, i.e. not so often. The above example is the only way of expressing ‘to have something’.


When we have more or less than one, e.g. 0, 2, 3, 15, 256 etc., we use the partitive:

Minulla on puhelin (I have a phone)
Minulla on kaksi puhelinta (I have two phones)
Minulla ei ole puhelinta (I don’t have a phone)


^Numbers^

1: yksi
2: kaksi
3: kolme
4: neljä
5: viisi
6: kuusi
7: seitsemän
8: kahdeksan
9: yhdeksän
10: kymmenen
11: yksitoista

Etymologically, the words for 8 and 9 are probably so similar to 1 and 2 because their distance to number ten is two and one, respectively (compare *kaksi* and *kahdeksan*, *yksi* and *yhdeksän*. This makes it easier to remember.


^Ordinal numbers^

first - ensimmäinen
second - toinen
third - kolmas
fourth - neljäs
fifth - viides
sixth - kuudes
seventh - seitsemäs
eighth - kahdeksas
ninth - yhdeksäs
tenth - kymmenes
eleventh - yhdestoista